1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,963
Visitors expect a site in their own language.

2
00:00:03,963 --> 00:00:08,959
In this video, you enable the localization features, choose the languages of the

3
00:00:08,959 --> 00:00:14,130
site, translate a page, and add a menu that lets visitors switch between languages.

4
00:00:18,730 --> 00:00:21,552
Let's build a small site in two languages.

5
00:00:21,552 --> 00:00:25,314
English is the default, and French is the second language.

6
00:00:25,314 --> 00:00:31,310
The About page gets a French translation, and a language menu lets visitors switch between them.

7
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:34,820
Three features make a site multilingual.

8
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:38,300
Localization defines the cultures the site supports.

9
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:42,620
A culture is a language, usually with a region, such as English in

10
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:46,460
the United States, and one of the cultures is the default.

11
00:00:47,210 --> 00:00:52,437
Content Localization lets a content item have one translation for each culture.

12
00:00:52,437 --> 00:00:56,864
Each translation is a content item of its own, with its own title,

13
00:00:56,864 --> 00:01:01,660
text, and permalink, and the translations of a page are linked together.

14
00:01:02,410 --> 00:01:07,176
Content Culture Picker provides a language menu for the navigation bar of the site.

15
00:01:07,176 --> 00:01:11,513
When a visitor chooses a language, the picker takes them to the translation

16
00:01:11,513 --> 00:01:14,190
of the page they are reading, if one exists.

17
00:01:18,490 --> 00:01:20,723
Let's start with the features.

18
00:01:20,723 --> 00:01:26,120
Open Tools, Features, and type localization in the search box to narrow the list.

19
00:01:26,617 --> 00:01:28,586
Find Content Culture Picker.

20
00:01:28,586 --> 00:01:35,478
Its row lists Content Localization among the features it depends on, and Content Localization needs Localization.

21
00:01:35,478 --> 00:01:38,837
Press Enable to turn on all three features together.

22
00:01:42,672 --> 00:01:44,812
Search for localization again:

23
00:01:44,812 --> 00:01:50,282
Localization, Content Localization, and Content Culture Picker are all marked Enabled.

24
00:01:54,315 --> 00:01:57,174
Open Settings, Localization, Cultures.

25
00:01:57,174 --> 00:02:02,631
The table starts with one culture, taken from the computer that runs the site.

26
00:02:02,631 --> 00:02:06,075
Here it is en-US, English in the United States.

27
00:02:06,615 --> 00:02:12,482
Below the table, choose fr-FR, French in France, in the list of cultures.

28
00:02:12,482 --> 00:02:16,195
Press Add culture, and a second row appears.

29
00:02:16,831 --> 00:02:20,040
Each row has a radio button in the Default culture column.

30
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:24,051
The selected one is the default, and a translatable content item starts in

31
00:02:24,051 --> 00:02:25,957
that culture when you create it.

32
00:02:25,957 --> 00:02:27,411
Leave English selected.

33
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:28,883
Press Save.

34
00:02:28,883 --> 00:02:33,602
The site restarts so that the settings take effect, and a message says so.

35
00:02:33,602 --> 00:02:37,260
After the restart, the site supports English and French.

36
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:45,741
A content type is translatable when it has the Localization part.

37
00:02:45,741 --> 00:02:47,508
Let's add it to Article.

38
00:02:47,508 --> 00:02:52,220
Open Design, Content Definition, Content Types, and press Edit on Article.

39
00:02:52,884 --> 00:02:54,969
Under Parts, press Add Parts.

40
00:02:54,969 --> 00:02:58,426
The list shows the parts you can attach to the type.

41
00:02:58,426 --> 00:03:01,764
Check Localization, then press Add Selected Parts.

42
00:03:02,231 --> 00:03:06,565
A message confirms that the part was added, and Localization appears in the

43
00:03:06,565 --> 00:03:07,849
parts of the type.

44
00:03:07,849 --> 00:03:08,759
Press Save.

45
00:03:08,759 --> 00:03:11,541
A type without this part cannot be translated.

46
00:03:15,841 --> 00:03:18,084
Let's translate the About page.

47
00:03:18,084 --> 00:03:21,601
Open Content, Content Items, and find the About row.

48
00:03:22,258 --> 00:03:26,389
Press Edit, then scroll down to the panel that begins with Current Culture.

49
00:03:26,389 --> 00:03:28,838
It names the culture of this content item.

50
00:03:29,301 --> 00:03:30,555
Open the panel.

51
00:03:30,555 --> 00:03:35,988
It asks you to save the content item before creating localizations, so press Publish.

52
00:03:35,988 --> 00:03:37,601
The list opens again.

53
00:03:40,824 --> 00:03:44,914
The About row shows a culture badge and a Localizations button.

54
00:03:44,914 --> 00:03:47,226
Open Localizations to see French.

55
00:03:47,226 --> 00:03:50,664
A plus sign marks a culture that has no translation.

56
00:03:51,201 --> 00:03:52,358
Choose French.

57
00:03:52,358 --> 00:03:56,986
Orchard Core creates a French copy of the page and opens it in the editor.

58
00:03:56,986 --> 00:04:01,441
The panel shows French as the current culture, and lists English with a

59
00:04:01,441 --> 00:04:03,871
pencil, a link back to the original.

60
00:04:04,326 --> 00:04:08,810
Change the title to À propos, give the page its own permalink, a-propos,

61
00:04:08,810 --> 00:04:11,626
and replace the text with a French version.

62
00:04:19,415 --> 00:04:20,706
Press Publish.

63
00:04:20,706 --> 00:04:22,771
The list shows both pages:

64
00:04:22,771 --> 00:04:25,611
About in en-US, and À propos in fr-FR.

65
00:04:25,611 --> 00:04:30,000
Each translation is a content item of its own, so you can edit

66
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,065
and publish it separately.

67
00:04:32,521 --> 00:04:35,444
The list also has a Culture filter.

68
00:04:35,444 --> 00:04:39,651
Choose fr-FR, and the list shows only French content.

69
00:04:43,951 --> 00:04:48,189
The picker appears in the navigation bar of themes that render it.

70
00:04:48,189 --> 00:04:51,602
The Blog Theme does not, and The Default Theme does.

71
00:04:51,602 --> 00:04:55,781
Open Design, Themes, and press Make Current on The Default Theme.

72
00:04:56,252 --> 00:04:58,398
A message confirms the change.

73
00:04:58,398 --> 00:05:02,212
Next, open Settings, Localization, Content Culture Picker.

74
00:05:02,212 --> 00:05:07,575
Set Cookie remembers the language a visitor chooses, and Homepage redirect makes the

75
00:05:07,575 --> 00:05:12,402
picker try the home page in that language when the page has no translation.

76
00:05:12,890 --> 00:05:16,579
Content Culture, next to it, has its own Set Cookie option.

77
00:05:16,579 --> 00:05:20,948
When it is on, opening a translated page also remembers the language of

78
00:05:20,948 --> 00:05:24,410
that page, for pages that have no culture of their own.

79
00:05:28,710 --> 00:05:31,632
Let's visit the site and open the About page.

80
00:05:31,632 --> 00:05:36,730
The navigation bar shows a menu with the current language, English (United States).

81
00:05:37,188 --> 00:05:38,305
Open the menu.

82
00:05:38,305 --> 00:05:41,318
It lists the other languages by their own names.

83
00:05:41,318 --> 00:05:43,048
Choose français (France).

84
00:05:44,315 --> 00:05:49,324
The picker takes the visitor to the French page, with its own title and text.

85
00:05:49,324 --> 00:05:53,005
The menu shows the current language, français (France).

86
00:05:53,557 --> 00:05:59,217
Choose English (United States) in the menu to go back to the About page, in English.

87
00:05:59,689 --> 00:06:02,943
A page without a translation behaves differently.

88
00:06:02,943 --> 00:06:08,209
On the home page, choose français (France), and the visitor stays on the same page.

89
00:06:12,409 --> 00:06:17,136
When a visitor chooses a language, the picker first looks for the translation

90
00:06:17,136 --> 00:06:18,617
of the current page.

91
00:06:18,617 --> 00:06:21,749
If there is one, the visitor goes straight to it.

92
00:06:22,499 --> 00:06:27,035
If the page has no translation and Homepage redirect is on, the picker

93
00:06:27,035 --> 00:06:29,959
tries the home page in the chosen language.

94
00:06:30,709 --> 00:06:34,517
In every other case, the visitor stays on the current page.

95
00:06:34,517 --> 00:06:38,969
When Set Cookie is on, the picker still remembers the chosen language.

96
00:06:40,969 --> 00:06:43,429
You have a site with two languages:

97
00:06:43,429 --> 00:06:47,569
cultures, a translatable type, a translated page, and a picker.

98
00:06:47,569 --> 00:06:53,569
The labels of the interface itself come from translation files, which a separate video covers.

99
00:06:53,569 --> 00:06:55,129
Thanks for watching.

